## Common patterns / recipes

### "I want a basic single-enemy attack"

Set the four physical-pipeline tags (`checks_spirit_gate`,
`requires_hit_roll`, `checks_guard`, `checks_stance`), set `hp_damage`,
and TARGETING = single_enemy. Damage source optional.

### "I want a discipline attack" (vampire/fera/wraith etc.)

Same as above but add `energy_cost`, drop the `requires_hit_roll`
and `checks_guard` tags so it's the supernatural pipeline (auto-hit,
ignores guard — keep `checks_stance` so dodge/stealth still evade),
and gate the decision on the relevant trait+perk. Often pair with a
`damage_source` flavor (fire for Lure of Flames, etc.) to make it
aggravated against vulnerable splats.

### "I want an AoE attack"

Switch TARGETING to `team_enemy`. The hp_damage value applies to *each*
enemy hit. Cost should reflect that — typically 5-6 AP and 2 energy.
Consider `bypass_dodge` on AoE moves so a single dodging enemy doesn't
dodge the whole sweep on themselves only.

### "I want a heal-on-hit (vampiric)"

Pair `hp_damage` with `hp_heal` and gate the heal on landing the hit by adding `POD_combat_move_set_heal_condition = { VALUE = on_hp_damage }`. Add `POD_combat_move_set_flag = { NAME = drain }` if you also want the tooltip to display "Drain 2" instead of "Heal 2". Without the heal_condition setter the heal fires unconditionally even on a miss / dodge / stealth-evade — turning the move into a damage-and-free-heal that's almost always worth using.

### "I want a defensive stance"

TARGETING = self, set the appropriate stance flag (`set_guard`,
`dodge`, or `stealth`). Don't combine with damage — stances are
"spend AP to be safer next turn" plays. Cost typically 2-3 AP.

### "I want a buff that lasts the whole fight"

Use `*_combat` tier setters instead of `*_round`. Combat-duration
buffs are pricier (they don't refresh) — typically 3-4 AP for a
medium tier or 4-5 AP for large.

### "I want a debuff against the target"

Use `_debuff_round` or `_debuff_combat` tier setters with TARGETING =
single_enemy. AI scoring already understands tier debuffs and will
pick them when stacking is favorable.

### "I want a counter-attack stance"

Set `set_guard` (or just leave actor unguarded for a riskier setup),
add `retaliate_if_hit` flag, and set `retaliate_damage`. The actor
counter-attacks anyone who hits them next turn. For an aggravated
counter,  `set_retaliate_agg = yes` 

### "I want a 'finishing' move that's only useful at low HP"

There's no built-in conditional damage scaling, but you can set high
hp_damage + high AP cost to make it a closer move naturally. The AI's
HP-finish bonus (+25 when target has ≤1 HP left) will already make
it pick this when it's relevant.

### "I want a cantrip that gives me extra AP"

TARGETING = self, set `ap_gain` parameter. Make sure to include some 
other cost. So, you can't infinitely generate AP.

